    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "a0e40d2027b556e689526dae62a0d8ce",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"convention-note-softmaxglmhmm-vs-dynamax\">Convention note: <code>SoftmaxGLMHMM</code> vs Dynamax</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Task: <strong>MCDR</strong></li>\n<li>Number of classes: <strong>3</strong></li>\n<li>Our <code>SoftmaxGLMHMM</code> uses <strong><code>(K, C-1, M)</code></strong> emission weights and <strong>the last class is the reference class</strong>.</li>\n<li>In this task that means the learned explicit contrasts are <strong>Left, Center</strong>, and <strong>Right</strong> is the implicit reference with logit <code>0</code>.</li>\n<li>So the notebook simulation uses <code>logits = [eta..., 0]</code>, matching the model exactly.</li>\n<li>Dynamax <code>CategoricalRegressionHMM</code> instead uses <strong>full <code>(K, C, M)</code> softmax weights</strong> with one explicit weight vector per class.</li>\n<li>The two parameterizations are equivalent only after embedding our weights into Dynamax's full-softmax form.</li>\n</ul></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
